PARS = [
("Decision analysis has long assumed", """Decision analysis takes the set of alternatives as given and concentrates on evaluating it {35;36}. This is the weak point for a DSS under time pressure. Option generation has been identified as a neglected phase that precedes choice {18}, formalized as a recursion of decision problems {19;37}, and shown to decide adoption, since planning systems that hard-wire the open aspects of a problem are not used {20}. Cognitive evidence agrees. Option generation in ill-structured problems depends on ideation beyond memory retrieval, more options raise the quality of the best option {38}, and operators build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from menus {23}. Hence, a DSS should generate alternatives during operation and filter them by a strict, external evaluation."""),
("Three kinds of mechanism", """Three kinds of mechanism exist for this. The first searches a fixed model for solutions that differ in structure, as in modeling to generate alternatives {21} and the generator-evaluator separation of active decision support {22}. The second combines modules under compatibility constraints {17;39}. The third adds new actions to the action model at run time, as in open-world planning and action discovery {40;41}. In all three the new alternatives are built from parts the system already understands, so an open decision space is an open composition over a closed and verified basis. The same holds for the mechanisms that change a rule base at run time. Evolving TS models tune and add rules within the vocabulary {33;34}, value-based selectors learn when a structural change is needed {42}, and generative models draft rules outside the current structure {43}. VODA uses all three. Tuning and selection run in the closed layer, generation runs in the open layer, and one satisficing acceptance rule {44} decides what enters, because generation must be cheap to reject and the evaluator must be independent of the generator."""),
]
